#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tk-context — how full is THIS session's context window, in tokens.

WHY IT EXISTS: `WINDOW.md` asks the orchestrator to read the context number at
every seam, and for three generations no orchestrator could. The number is in
the statusline, and the statusline is rendered to the user's terminal — it never
enters the model's context. Measured 2026-09-03: three generations in a row
reported the number as estimated, unread, or `n/m`, and the third then cited a
threshold it had never read. This command is the reading the rule always meant.

WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM: the session's own transcript. Claude Code records
the `usage` of every API response there, and the shell environment carries
`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`, which names the file. The occupancy is
`input_tokens + cache_creation_input_tokens + cache_read_input_tokens` — the
formula the official statusline documentation declares for
`context_window.total_input_tokens`. Cross-checked twice against a rendered
statusline on 2026-09-03: 290,286 against 293k, and 86k → 90k → 98k across one
session's calls.

ABSOLUTE, NEVER A PERCENTAGE. The threshold this feeds measures the SMART ZONE —
judgement degrading — not the window running out, so it does not scale with the
window. On a 1M-context model 293k reads as 29%, and an orchestrator comparing
that against "~150-200k" concludes it has room while sitting well past the
ceiling. So this command prints tokens and no fraction.

TWO RECORD CLASSES DECIDE THE OCCUPANCY, and the LAST RECORDED of them wins: an
API response, which carries `message.usage`, and a COMPACT BOUNDARY, which
carries none. Reading only the first is how a filter over this file lies:
measured on a real transcript, the last usage row said 263,729 and the boundary
written after it said `postTokens: 10340` — the window had been emptied and the
reader walked past the record saying so, exit 0, no signal. Six transcripts in a
population of 299 carry a boundary, and every one is overstated the same way.
Each pair below is what a usage-only reader WOULD HAVE PRINTED against what the
boundary said, measured record by record — not the boundary's own `preTokens`,
which is a different number a few hundred tokens higher:

    768,430 / 10,803 · 498,097 / 23,198 · 374,302 / 13,572
    282,107 / 11,771 · 263,729 / 10,340 · 241,979 / 24,193

This is why the walk below reads record CLASSES rather than filtering for the
shape it likes. Enumerating every other type is not the goal and would be
endless — the rule is "what changes the occupancy", and a class added to that
set is added here.

LAST RECORDED, not newest by timestamp. The file is append-only, so its order is
the order the records happened; the `timestamp` carried alongside is for the
reader of the curve and decides nothing.

HOW STALE THE NUMBER IS. Between an API response and the next, it lags the live
statusline by about one turn, and lags it LOW. Across a compaction it does not
lag at all: the boundary is exact and the reading before it was another window's.

WHAT IT DOES NOT SEE: subagents. Their transcripts are not in the parent's file
(measured: zero `isSidechain` entries across 299 transcripts, subagents
dispatched throughout), and their windows are their own. The `subagent_tokens` a
run reports measure that run, not this session. Run from INSIDE a subagent the
command therefore reports the PARENT's number, frozen at the parent's last turn
— measured, unchanged over eight minutes and a dozen subagent turns. Nothing
warns of it, because nothing can: `CLAUDE_CODE_CHILD_SESSION=1` is set in the
MAIN session's shell too (measured), so it marks a child PROCESS and not a
subagent, and a guard on it would fire on every ordinary call. No prose asks a
subagent for this number; a subagent that wants one asks its parent.

THE WINDOW IS THE HARNESS'S, AND `--window` ONLY REPORTS IT. The compaction
this session will meet is the harness's own, fixed by the `autoCompactWindow`
key (settings user, project or local) with the environment variable
`CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW` winning over it, and the threshold is the
window less the buffer the summary needs. All three were read out of the
shipped binary on 2026-09-09 (2.1.266): the resolver takes the environment
first and the settings key second, both bounded to 100,000-1,000,000 tokens,
and the threshold is `window - min(max output tokens, 20,000)`.

WHY A CHANNEL OF ITS OWN, and not one wider line. The bare invocation is read
by seams that compare or pipe it, so stdout stays `<n> tokens in context` and
nothing else; the window and the threshold go to stderr, as `--curve` does.

NO KEY IS NOT "NO NUMBER". Exit 2 means one thing here — the transcript holds
no token count — and `WINDOW.md` spends it as a licence to decide by judgement.
An unconfigured `autoCompactWindow` is not that: the harness still has a window,
`--window` prints the resolution it falls back to, MARKED as the default, and
the exit stays 0.

Usage:  tk-context [--curve] [--window] [--session ID] [--transcript PATH]
Exit:   0 the number is printed · 2 no number is available · 64 bad usage.
        The three are distinct on purpose: invariant of the seam calling this,
        a mistyped flag must not read as "no number, decide by judgement".
"""

import argparse
import glob
import json
import os
import re
import sys
from typing import NamedTuple

EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_NO_NUMBER = 2
EXIT_USAGE = 64                       # sysexits.h EX_USAGE; argparse's own 2 collides

# The three input counters the window holds. Output tokens are excluded on
# purpose: the documented `used_percentage` is input-only, and matching it is
# what lets this number be compared against a statusline reading.
INPUT_FIELDS = ("input_tokens", "cache_creation_input_tokens",
                "cache_read_input_tokens")

CURVE_ROWS = 6                        # how many samples of the curve are shown

# The harness's own compaction window, read from the shipped binary (2.1.266) on
# 2026-09-09 rather than assumed. The environment variable is consulted first and
# the settings key second; either is clamped into the same bounds, and the
# threshold subtracts the room the summary itself needs.
WINDOW_ENV = "CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW"
WINDOW_KEY = "autoCompactWindow"
WINDOW_FLOOR = 100_000                # the resolver's own lower bound
WINDOW_CEILING = 1_000_000            # its upper bound, and the `auto` resolution
WINDOW_AUTO = "auto"                  # the one word the key takes instead of a number
# `auto` resolves to the model's own context. Measured 1,000,000 on Fable, which
# is the model an orchestrator runs; it is printed as the DEFAULT and never as a
# reading, because no key was consulted to get it.
WINDOW_DEFAULT = 1_000_000
# `window - min(max output tokens, 20_000)`. The cap is what binds on every model
# this house dispatches, so the buffer is the cap.
SUMMARY_BUFFER = 20_000

# Where the merged settings live, in the order the harness merges them: the later
# file wins. The project pair is resolved from the CWD, and the harness resolves
# it from the directory the session was opened in — the two agree while the caller
# runs this from that directory, and the tick of `WINDOW.md` does. Read from
# anywhere else, the user file is still seen and the project pair is missed, which
# understates nothing: an unreadable key falls through to the default and says so.
SETTINGS_FILES = ("~/.claude/settings.json",
                  ".claude/settings.json",
                  ".claude/settings.local.json")


class Occupancy(NamedTuple):
    """One record's answer about the window. `kind` says which class said it,
    and the caller tells a boundary's number from a response's by reading it."""

    stamp: str
    tokens: int
    kind: str


def plain(value):
    """A value from outside this process, safe to print to a terminal.

    Four things this command prints came from somewhere else: the session id
    (the environment, or an operator's `--session`), the paths that matched, and
    a boundary's timestamp and a record's uuid, both read out of the jsonl. None
    of them is attacker-controlled in any ordinary use, and the cost of being
    wrong about that is a terminal a control sequence reprograms — measured in
    this house on a sibling channel, where a directory NAME carried the escape.
    So the sequences are stripped where the value enters the output, not trusted
    at their source.
    """
    return re.sub(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f]", "?", str(value))


def no_number(msg):
    """Stop with code 2, and say what the caller owes instead of the number.

    The message is half the point. A seam that cannot read the number is not
    licensed to invent one: it decides by judgement and SAYS it is judgement,
    which is the failure this whole command exists to answer.
    """
    print(f"tk-context: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    print("tk-context: no number — decide by judgement, and say it is judgement",
          file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(EXIT_NO_NUMBER)


def find_transcript(session_id):
    """The jsonl for one session id, under any project directory.

    The id is ESCAPED before it reaches the glob. Unescaped, `--session '*'`
    matched a stranger's transcript and printed its number at exit 0 — a
    confident wrong answer, which is the one outcome this command may not give.
    Two files for one id is refused rather than resolved: sorting picks a winner
    silently, and the loser is as likely to be the live session as the stale one.
    """
    root = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
    hits = sorted(glob.glob(os.path.join(root, "*", f"{glob.escape(session_id)}.jsonl")))
    if not hits:
        no_number(f"no transcript for session {plain(session_id)} under {root}")
    if len(hits) > 1:
        no_number("two transcripts carry this session id: "
                  + ", ".join(plain(h) for h in hits))
    return hits[0]


def response_tokens(record):
    """The window after an API response, or None if this is not one."""
    usage = (record.get("message") or {}).get("usage")
    if not isinstance(usage, dict):
        return None
    try:
        total = sum(int(usage.get(f) or 0) for f in INPUT_FIELDS)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        # A counter that is not a number: refuse. Coercing or skipping would
        # report an OLDER window as though it were current, which is a wrong
        # number, and this command's whole contract is that it never gives one.
        no_number("a usage counter in "
                  f"{plain(record.get('uuid') or 'a record')} is not a number")
    # Zero across all three is not an occupancy. Synthetic replies carry one —
    # the rate-limit message is written with an all-zero usage block — and
    # taking it would print `0 tokens in context` over a real 190k window.
    return total or None


def boundary_tokens(record):
    """The window after a compaction, or None if this is not a boundary.

    A boundary whose count cannot be read REFUSES, exactly as a response with an
    unreadable counter does. Returning None here instead would hand the answer
    back to the pre-compaction row and print it at exit 0 — the very defect this
    class was added to close, walked back in through the malformed case. What is
    known in that state is that the window was emptied; what is not known is to
    what, and there is no number to give.
    """
    if record.get("subtype") != "compact_boundary":
        return None
    post = (record.get("compactMetadata") or {}).get("postTokens")
    if not isinstance(post, int):
        no_number(f"the compaction at {plain(record.get('timestamp') or '?')} "
                  "records no readable postTokens")
    return post


def occupancies(path):
    """Every recorded occupancy of this session's window, oldest first.

    Read line by line: a long session's transcript reaches megabytes (measured:
    81 ms on 6.3 MB), and only the counters are wanted. Malformed lines are
    skipped rather than fatal — a transcript is appended to live, so the last
    line can be half-written.

    Each record is asked, in turn, what it says about the window. A record that
    answers nothing is not an error and not an occupancy; it is the other 99% of
    the file.
    """
    out = []
    try:
        handle = open(path, errors="replace")
    except OSError as exc:
        no_number(f"cannot read {path}: {exc}")
    with handle:
        for line in handle:
            try:
                record = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            # Belt and braces: subagent entries are not written here today, and
            # the file being keyed by this session's id is the real guarantee.
            if record.get("isSidechain"):
                continue
            for kind, reader in (("response", response_tokens),
                                 ("compaction", boundary_tokens)):
                total = reader(record)
                if total is not None:
                    out.append(Occupancy(record.get("timestamp") or "", total, kind))
                    break
    return out


def settings_value():
    """`autoCompactWindow` from the merged settings, and the file it came from.

    Guarding the parse is not guarding the open: a settings path can be a
    directory, can hold a byte-order mark glued to the first key, and can be a
    FIFO that never returns. Each file is confirmed a regular file, opened as
    `utf-8-sig`, and every way it can fail is caught — a settings file nobody
    can read must not take down the number the caller actually asked for.
    """
    found = (None, None)
    for name in SETTINGS_FILES:
        path = os.path.expanduser(name)
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            continue
        try:
            with open(path, encoding="utf-8-sig") as handle:
                payload = json.load(handle)
        except (OSError, ValueError, UnicodeDecodeError):
            continue
        if isinstance(payload, dict) and WINDOW_KEY in payload:
            found = (payload[WINDOW_KEY], path)
    return found


def effective_window():
    """(tokens, where it came from, whether it was configured at all).

    The order is the harness's: the environment variable beats the key, the key
    beats the default. A value outside the bounds, or a word other than `auto`,
    is not obeyed — the harness refuses it too — and the resolution falls
    through to the next source rather than inventing one. `auto` IS obeyed: it
    is a configured key, reported as one, and its number is the model's own
    context — measured 1M on Fable, which is what `WINDOW_DEFAULT` holds.
    """
    raw = os.environ.get(WINDOW_ENV)
    if raw is not None:
        try:
            asked = int(str(raw).strip())
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            asked = None
        if asked is not None and WINDOW_FLOOR <= asked <= WINDOW_CEILING:
            return asked, f"the environment ({WINDOW_ENV})", True

    value, path = settings_value()
    if isinstance(value, bool):
        value = None                  # `isinstance(True, int)` is true in Python
    if isinstance(value, int) and WINDOW_FLOOR <= value <= WINDOW_CEILING:
        return value, f"`{WINDOW_KEY}` in {plain(path)}", True
    if isinstance(value, str) and value.strip() == WINDOW_AUTO:
        return (WINDOW_DEFAULT,
                f"`{WINDOW_KEY}` = {WINDOW_AUTO} in {plain(path)}", True)

    return WINDOW_DEFAULT, "the harness DEFAULT", False


def print_window():
    """The window and the threshold, on stderr, said as what they are.

    Two lines and not one: a seam that reads the window without the threshold
    compares its tokens against the wrong number, and the difference is the
    whole buffer.
    """
    window, source, configured = effective_window()
    said = f"window {window} tokens, from {source}"
    if not configured:
        said += (f" — no {WINDOW_ENV} and no readable `{WINDOW_KEY}`; `auto` "
                 f"measured {WINDOW_DEFAULT} on Fable")
    print(f"tk-context: {said}", file=sys.stderr)
    print(f"tk-context: the harness compacts above {window - SUMMARY_BUFFER} "
          f"tokens (window less {SUMMARY_BUFFER} of summary buffer)",
          file=sys.stderr)


def print_curve(points):
    """The slope, not just the height.

    A seam reading one number cannot tell 15k a step from 40k a step, and it is
    the slope that decides whether another review fits before the ceiling.
    """
    if len(points) < 2:
        return
    step = max(1, (len(points) - 1) // (CURVE_ROWS - 1))
    sampled = points[::step]
    if sampled[-1] is not points[-1]:
        sampled.append(points[-1])
    print("curve (oldest first):", file=sys.stderr)
    previous = None
    for point in sampled:
        delta = "" if previous is None else f"  {point.tokens - previous:+d}"
        mark = "  <- compaction" if point.kind == "compaction" else ""
        stamp = plain(point.stamp)[:19] or "?"
        print(f"  {stamp:19}  {point.tokens:>9,d}{delta}{mark}", file=sys.stderr)
        previous = point.tokens


class Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    """Usage errors exit 64, not argparse's 2.

    2 is this command's "there is no number, decide by judgement". A mistyped
    flag arriving at a seam as that message is the one confusion invariant the
    exit codes exist to prevent.
    """

    def error(self, message):
        self.print_usage(sys.stderr)
        print(f"{self.prog}: error: {message}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(EXIT_USAGE)


def main(argv=None):
    parser = Parser(
        description="print this session's context occupancy, in tokens")
    parser.add_argument("--curve", action="store_true",
                        help="also print the occupancy over the session, on stderr")
    parser.add_argument("--window", action="store_true",
                        help="also print the harness's compaction window and "
                             "threshold, on stderr")
    parser.add_argument("--session", default=os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID"),
                        help="session id (default: $CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID)")
    parser.add_argument("--transcript",
                        help="read this jsonl instead of resolving the session id")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    path = args.transcript
    if not path:
        if not args.session:
            no_number("no CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID in the environment")
        path = find_transcript(args.session)

    points = occupancies(path)
    if not points:
        no_number(f"no API response recorded yet in {path}")

    last = points[-1]
    print(f"{last.tokens} tokens in context")
    if last.kind == "compaction":
        # The window was emptied after the last response, and this number is the
        # boundary's own. Said aloud because it is the case a reader of the old
        # filter never saw: it printed the pre-compaction window and looked sure.
        print(f"tk-context: read from the compaction at {plain(last.stamp)[:19]}, "
              "not from an API response", file=sys.stderr)
    if args.window:
        print_window()
    if args.curve:
        print_curve(points)
    return EXIT_OK


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
